    "result_text": "There's only one test class and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule so that a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date is rejected with a 400, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `rest/escalation/FutureRegistrationDateException.java` \u2014 a checked exception carrying the rejected date.\n- `rest/escalation/FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400` with a `ProblemDetail` (using the shared `ProblemDetails` builder and a `rejectedValue` property, mirroring the existing `InvalidPostcode` handler).\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n- `rest/function/owner/ValidateRegistrationDate.java` \u2014 a small single-responsibility step that reads the validated `OwnerFieldsDto`, skips when no date is supplied, and throws when `supplied.isAfter(LocalDate.now())`.\n\n**Wiring:**\n- Inserted a `validateRegistrationDate` step into `owners.POST.yml` right after `validate` (so the future-date check runs early, before `BuildOwner`), keeping the `govern: [ transaction ]` pattern consistent with the surrounding steps.\n\nThe check operates on the raw *supplied* date (per the spec \u2014 \"later than the server date\"), leaving the existing default/business-day roll-forward logic in `RegistrationDates`/`ResolveRegistrationDate` untouched. The optional-when-omitted behavior is preserved (null \u2192 skip). Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
